Chapter 18 — Part 1
Curtis reached the building just as the afternoon light started to soften. Hina and Yui waited on the front steps with an older man in a simple button-down and work trousers. The landlord looked up when Curtis approached, taking in the height, the braids, and the bandaged hand.
“You the one asking about the basement?”
“Yes, sir.” Curtis stopped a polite distance away and bowed once. “Curtis Beardy. Hina said you had a few minutes.”
The landlord nodded, already turning toward the side entrance. “It’s small. Private door. Bathroom, kitchenette, one main room. Rent’s posted. You working?”
“Two jobs. School during the day.” Curtis followed, Hina and Yui falling in behind. “I can pay the deposit today if the place is sound.”
“We’ll see.” The man unlocked the lower door and pushed it open. Cool air and the smell of clean concrete drifted out. “Take a look. Girls can come too if they’re vouching.”
Hina shot Curtis a small, hopeful smile as they stepped down. Yui lingered at the threshold, arms crossed, watching both of them. The low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest stayed quiet while the landlord flipped on the lights and the empty suite waited to be judged.
The landlord swept his flashlight across the main room. Stains on the concrete. A cracked cabinet door hanging crooked. One window that didn’t quite seal. “Last tenant left it in bad shape. Didn’t get the deposit back. I haven’t had time to fix it proper.” He glanced at Curtis. “Still yours if you want it. Rent stays the same. You just live with the mess until I get around to it.”
Curtis looked over the space—bare, scuffed, quiet. He nodded once. “It’s not bad. I’ve slept in a burning forest. Is it alright if I take it upon myself to fix this suite?”
The older man raised a brow. “You handy?”
“Enough. I can patch, paint, replace what’s broken. Won’t charge you for the labor. Just rather have it livable sooner.”
Hina’s eyes brightened. Yui hid a small smile behind her hand. The landlord studied Curtis for another second, then grunted. “Fine. Don’t knock down any walls. You break it worse, you buy it. Deal’s the same otherwise.”
“Deal.” Curtis extended his good hand. The landlord shook it. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the empty suite suddenly had a future and the two girls exchanged a look behind his back.
They handled the paperwork at the small table in the suite’s kitchenette. Curtis counted out the deposit in clean bills, then unzipped the duffel and set the whole bag on the table. Stacks of yen filled it—club pay, odd jobs, what he’d brought from before.
“This should cover a year, yeah?”
The landlord stared. He opened the bag wider, thumbed a stack, and looked up like Curtis had just set a live animal in front of him.
“Beardy. What the hell.”
Hina’s mouth had fallen open. Yui leaned in, eyes wide. “Is that—all cash?”
“Easier than transfers,” Curtis said, expression even. “I don’t have a local bank set up yet. If it’s too much at once, I can do months. But I’d rather have it handled.”
The landlord rubbed his face, then laughed once, short and disbelieving. “Kid, most tenants argue over the deposit. You’re trying to prepay a year out of a gym bag.” He zipped it halfway and pushed it back. “I’ll take three months upfront. Rest you keep. And I’m still not fixing the cabinet.”
Curtis nodded, pulled three stacks, and left the rest. “Fair.”
Hina was still staring at the bag. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the landlord wrote a receipt and the basement suite officially stopped being empty.
Curtis kept cutting. The saw moved in clean strokes. Sweat ran down his chest, over the ridges of his stomach, and disappeared into the waistband of the compression pants sitting low on his hips. Every time he reached for another board the muscles in his back shifted. He was still humming, still completely focused on the wood.
