Curtis moved in and out of the suite all evening. Fresh boards went in. The cracked cabinet door came off and a new one went on. He sealed the window, scrubbed the concrete until the worst stains faded, and ran a coat of clean paint across the worst walls. The humming never fully stopped.
By night the difference was obvious.
The main room no longer looked abandoned. The floor was swept and sealed. The kitchenette had a working counter and a cabinet that actually closed. A simple bed frame he’d built from the leftover lumber sat against the far wall with a new mattress still in its plastic. The bathroom door hung straight. A single lamp he’d picked up on one of the supply runs threw warm light across everything. It was still small. Still plain. But it was livable. It was his.
He stood in the doorway for a second, shirt back on, bandaged hand resting on the frame, and nodded once to himself. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet. Outside, the street had finally emptied. He stepped in, shut the door behind him, and locked it—properly this time—while the suite settled around the fact that someone was going to sleep in it for real.
Curtis sliced the tape on the first box with a pocket knife. A compact Bluetooth speaker sat inside, still wrapped. He set it on the new counter, paired it to his cracked phone, and hit play.
My Life Is a Party by the Italobrothers kicked through the small room—bright, electronic, too big for the space. He let it run.
The second box held square soundproof panels. He pulled the first one free, checked the adhesive, and pressed it into place on the wall beside the bed. Then another. The music bounced off the bare concrete until the panels started catching it. He worked steady, humming along under his breath, bandaged hand careful with the edges.
By the time the chorus hit again the worst of the echo had already softened. Curtis stepped back, wiped his hands on his pants, and nodded at the half-finished wall while the song kept playing and the suite finally started to feel like somewhere he could close the door and stay.
Hina stepped out of her room at the same time Yui opened hers. Both of them froze in the hallway.
Hina’s hair was still damp at the temples. Yui’s shirt was inside out. The flush on their faces hadn’t faded, and the look they exchanged said everything neither of them was going to admit out loud. They’d both just gotten off thinking about the same person. They both knew it.
Yui cleared her throat, cheeks burning. “Checking on him?”
“Yeah.” Hina tugged her sleeves down, equally red. “Make sure he didn’t nail his hand to the wall or something.”
They didn’t laugh. They just started down the stairs together, shoulders almost brushing, the shared knowledge sitting hot between them while the music from the basement drifted up to meet them. By the time they reached Curtis’s door the blush still hadn’t left either of their faces.
They reached the basement door still flushed, still not quite looking at each other. The music only registered when they stopped in front of it.
My life is a party, my home is the club, I party like a rockstar…
The Italobrothers track thumped clear through the wood, bright and unapologetic. Hina’s mouth twitched. Yui huffed a quiet laugh despite the heat still sitting in her face.
“He’s really playing that,” Yui said.
“While he hangs soundproofing,” Hina answered. She raised her hand, hesitated, then knocked. The song kept going on the other side. “Curtis? It’s us.”
Footsteps moved inside. The volume dipped. The lock turned. When the door opened he stood there in the compression clothes again, bandaged hand loose, a half-mounted panel visible behind him and the speaker still glowing on the counter.
“Hey,” he said, easy. “Come in. It’s louder than it looks.”
They stepped inside together, the chorus still humming under the sudden quiet, both of them very aware of how small the suite was and how close he was standing.
Curtis turned back to the kitchenette counter like the interruption had only paused him. The song was still going. He hummed along under his breath, nodding his head to the beat, a glass of something clear in his free hand. Vegetables were already piled beside a cutting board.
He took a drink, set the glass down, and spun on his heel. The knife left his fingers, flipped once, and hung in the air. He grabbed a fresh pepper from the pile with the same hand, turned back, and caught the knife on the way down without looking. The blade hit the board in a rapid, even rhythm—chop, chop, chop—perfectly in time with the music. He never glanced at his hands. Just kept nodding, humming, and working through the pile like the whole thing was a warm-up.
Hina and Yui stood just inside the door, watching. The flush on both their faces had climbed again. Every precise catch and cut made the low heat under their skin worse. Neither of them spoke. They just stared while Curtis finished the pepper, swept it aside with the flat of the knife, and reached for the next one, still perfectly on beat.
Curtis slid two bowls across the small counter. Rice, the vegetables he’d just cut, a simple sauce that smelled better than the suite had any right to produce. “Since you’re both here, enjoy.”
Hina and Yui picked up the chopsticks he’d set out, still flushed, still hyper-aware of how close the three of them were in the tiny kitchenette. They were halfway to the first bite when Curtis stepped back, bowed his head, and closed his eyes.
His voice came low and even.
“Lord, thanks for the food, the roof, and the company. Amen.”
He opened his eyes, picked up his own bowl, and started eating like nothing unusual had happened.
Both girls froze with chopsticks halfway to their mouths. Hina’s blush deepened for a completely different reason. Yui blinked, thrown. Neither of them had expected the quiet prayer. The music still played softly in the background. Curtis kept eating, expression easy, while the two of them slowly remembered their own food and tried not to stare at the boy who’d just thanked God in the middle of a basement suite he’d fixed himself.
Hina lowered her chopsticks, cheeks still warm. “You’re religious?”
Curtis chuckled, low and easy, and took another bite. “Probably an odd surprise considering where I’m from. But yes. To some degree.”
Yui glanced between them, curious. “Mountains and cryptids didn’t really scream Sunday service.”
“Didn’t have many services,” he said. “Still had reasons to be grateful. Food. Not dying. A door that locks.” He shrugged one shoulder. “Doesn’t come up much. Figured I’d say it since you were both here.”
Hina’s expression softened. She picked her chopsticks back up, the surprise settling into something quieter. “It’s nice. Most people our age don’t.”
“Most people our age didn’t grow up eating whatever didn’t eat them first.” His tone stayed light. “Eat. It’s better hot.”
They did. The music kept playing low. The three of them stood around the small counter in the half-finished suite, the prayer already fading into the ordinary rhythm of a shared meal, while the low, steady pull in Curtis’s chest remained as quiet as ever.
The Italobrothers track faded. The next song queued automatically. A sparse beat, then Curtis’s own voice—lower, rawer—came through the speaker.
I have no motivation, I don’t even wanna try

