He reached over and skipped it before the chorus could land. The speaker jumped to something instrumental and wordless. He went back to his bowl like the switch had been casual.
Yui’s chopsticks paused. “That was your voice.”
“Indeed.” Curtis didn’t look up. “But my music’s not light-hearted. Didn’t figure it fit the room.”
Hina’s expression had gone soft. She glanced at the speaker, then at him. “It sounded… honest.”
“Honest gets heavy fast.” He shrugged one shoulder, tone still even. “You two are eating. No need to put that on the meal.”
They let it sit. The instrumental filled the small suite. Yui took another bite, thoughtful. Hina watched him a second longer than necessary, the earlier heat still there but quieter now, while Curtis kept eating and the skipped song stayed unplayed between them.
They finished the bowls in quieter stretches. The instrumental kept looping low. Hina set her chopsticks down first and glanced at the half-finished soundproofing.
“You’re really staying here.”
“That’s the plan.” Curtis rinsed his bowl in the tiny sink. “Closer to the station. Real door. Beats the café booths.”
Yui leaned against the counter, arms crossed, the flush finally easing into something steadier. “And you’re fixing it yourself. Most people would’ve just complained to the landlord.”
“Complaining doesn’t patch a window.” He dried his hands on a towel and nodded toward the door. “You two should head up. It’s late. I’ve still got panels to hang.”
Hina hesitated, then smiled small. “Text if you need anything. We’re right upstairs.”
“Will do.”
They slipped out together. The door clicked shut behind them. Curtis turned the music back up—just enough to cover the hum of the building—and reached for the next panel. The low, steady pull in his chest stayed quiet while the suite settled around him and the night kept moving outside.
